```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int main() {
    char c[500];
    int score[26], key, i, j, top = 10;
    char p[500];

    printf("Enter ciphertext: ");
    fgets(c, sizeof(c), stdin);

    /* English letter frequency */
    int freq[26] = {
        82,15,28,43,127,22,20,61,70,15,8,40,24,
        67,75,19,1,60,63,91,28,10,24,2,20,1
    };

    /* Try all 26 possible keys */
    for(key = 0; key < 26; key++) {
        score[key] = 0;

        for(i = 0; c[i] != '\0'; i++) {
            if(isalpha(c[i])) {
                int x = toupper(c[i]) - 'A';
                int y = (x - key + 26) % 26;
                p[i] = y + 'A';
                score[key] += freq[y];
            } else
                p[i] = c[i];
        }
        p[i] = '\0';
    }

    /* Display top 10 keys */
    printf("\nTop 10 possible plaintexts:\n");

    for(i = 0; i < top; i++) {
        int best = -1;

        for(j = 0; j < 26; j++)
            if(score[j] >= 0 &&
               (best == -1 || score[j] > score[best]))
                best = j;

        printf("\nKey = %d, Score = %d\n", best, score[best]);

        for(j = 0; c[j] != '\0'; j++) {
            if(isalpha(c[j])) {
                int x = toupper(c[j]) - 'A';
                p[j] = (x - best + 26) % 26 + 'A';
            } else
                p[j] = c[j];
        }
        p[j] = '\0';

        printf("Plaintext: %s\n", p);

        score[best] = -1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

1. Tries all **26 possible additive-cipher keys**.
2. Decrypts the ciphertext using each key.
3. Uses standard **English letter-frequency values** to calculate a score.
4. Sorts the results by score.
5. Displays the **top 10 possible plaintexts** automatically.

For an additive cipher:

[
P=(C-K)\mod26
]

So the program requires **no manual key selection**.
